The four pieces wired together, verified (v6):

```tsx
// 1. PROVIDER — owns the location, syncs with the address bar
<BrowserRouter>
  <App />
</BrowserRouter>

// 2. MATCHER + 3. RULES — Routes renders the ONE best match
<Routes>
  <Route path="/" element={<Home />} />
  <Route path="/dashboard" element={<Dashboard />} />   {/* more specific beats order */}
  <Route path="*" element={<NotFound />} />              {/* splat = lowest rank, the fallback */}
</Routes>

// 4. NAVIGATION — a real <a> that routes instead of reloading
<Link to="/dashboard">Dashboard</Link>       {/* href="/dashboard" under the hood */}
<NavLink to="/dashboard" className={({ isActive }) => isActive ? 'active' : ''}>
  Dashboard
</NavLink>  {/* NavLink = Link + active-state styling hook */}
```

The matching model (the interview core): Routes takes the current location (from the provider context) and scores every Route — static segments rank higher than dynamic (:id) which rank higher than splats (*). The HIGHEST score renders; the others render nothing (unlike v5 Switch semantics that stopped at the first match — v6 rates, it doesn't scan). This is why order doesn't matter and why a splat at the bottom is the not-found fallback by rank, not by position.

Why Link instead of <a> (the answer interviewers want): a plain anchor triggers a full document navigation — the browser tears down the SPA, React state, and in-memory cache all die, then the whole bundle re-bootstraps. Link renders a genuine <a href> (so cmd-click/new-tab/middle-click and crawlers work) but intercepts the click and pushes through the History API — location updates, the provider re-renders its context, Routes re-matches, the new element renders. Same story for redirects: use <Navigate to="..." replace /> (declarative) or navigate('/x') from useNavigate() (imperative).
